/// <p>Since QAnimationGroup inherits from <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qabstractanimation.html" translate="no">QAbstractAnimation</a>, you can combine groups, and easily construct complex animation graphs. You can query <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qabstractanimation.html" translate="no">QAbstractAnimation</a> for the group it belongs to (using the <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qabstractanimation.html#group" translate="no">group</a>() function).</p>
/// <p>To start a top-level animation group, you simply use the <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qabstractanimation.html#start" translate="no">start</a>() function from <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qabstractanimation.html" translate="no">QAbstractAnimation</a>. By a top-level animation group, we think of a group that itself is not contained within another group. Starting sub groups directly is not supported, and may lead to unexpected behavior.</p>
/// <p>QAnimationGroup provides methods for adding and retrieving animations. Besides that, you can remove animations by calling <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qanimationgroup.html#removeAnimation" translate="no">removeAnimation</a>(), and clear the animation group by calling <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qanimationgroup.html#clear" translate="no">clear</a>(). You may keep track of changes in the group's animations by listening to <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qevent.html#Type-enum" translate="no">QEvent::ChildAdded</a> and <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qevent.html#Type-enum" translate="no">QEvent::ChildRemoved</a> events.</p>
