/// <p>The purpose of a QMutex is to protect an object, data structure or section of code so that only one thread can access it at a time (this is similar to the Java <code translate="no">synchronized</code> keyword). It is usually best to use a mutex with a <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qmutexlocker.html" translate="no">QMutexLocker</a> since this makes it easy to ensure that locking and unlocking are performed consistently.</p>
///
/// C++ class: <span style='color: green;'>```QMutex```</span>.
///
/// <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qmutex.html">C++ documentation</a>:<div style='border: 1px solid #5CFF95; background: #D6FFE4; padding: 16px;'><p>The purpose of a QMutex is to protect an object, data structure or section of code so that only one thread can access it at a time (this is similar to the Java <code translate="no">synchronized</code> keyword). It is usually best to use a mutex with a <a href="http://doc.qt.io/qt-5/qmutexlocker.html" translate="no">QMutexLocker</a> since this makes it easy to ensure that locking and unlocking are performed consistently.</p>
/// <p>For example, say there is a method that prints a message to the user on two lines:</p>
/// <pre class="cpp" translate="no"> <span class="type">int</span> number <span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">6</span>;
///
///  <span class="type">void</span> method1()
///  {
/// &#32;    number <span class="operator">*</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">5</span>;
/// &#32;    number <span class="operator">/</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">4</span>;
///  }
///
///  <span class="type">void</span> method2()
///  {
/// &#32;    number <span class="operator">*</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">3</span>;
/// &#32;    number <span class="operator">/</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">2</span>;
///  }
/// </pre>
/// <p>If these two methods are called in succession, the following happens:</p>
/// <pre class="cpp" translate="no"> <span class="comment">// method1()</span>
///  number <span class="operator">*</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">5</span>;        <span class="comment">// number is now 30</span>
///  number <span class="operator">/</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">4</span>;        <span class="comment">// number is now 7</span>
///
///  <span class="comment">// method2()</span>
///  number <span class="operator">*</span><span class="operator">=</span> <span class="number">3</span>;        <span class="comment">// number is now 21</span>
